{"id": "live-384", "title": "GSL Holdings, LLC v. Charter Township of Lyon, Michigan (Sixth Circuit Rejects Developer's Civil-Rights Claims Over Permitting Delay)", "category": "zoning-land-use", "status": "ruling", "date": "2026-11-09", "jurisdiction": "U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit", "state": "MI", "amount": "No dollar figure stated", "source": "live", "source_url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/11057877/gsl-holdings-llc-v-charter-twp-of-lyon-twp-mich/", "summary": "The Sixth Circuit, in a published opinion by Circuit Judge Andre B. Mathis filed November 9, 2026, affirmed summary judgment against GSL Holdings, LLC, a Michigan developer that sued Lyon Township and its supervisor under 42 U.S.C. Section 1983 after permitting delays over an on-site septic system requirement prevented GSL from meeting a build-to-suit occupancy deadline, causing its tenant, Moba Americas, Inc., to terminate the lease. The court held the township's insistence on a completed septic permit and county lot-combination before issuing a building permit did not amount to an equal-protection, due-process, or First Amendment violation.", "significance": "The ruling shows how difficult it is for a commercial developer to convert a frustrating, deal-killing municipal permitting dispute into a viable federal civil-rights claim, even where a county-level prerequisite outside the municipality's direct control is what ultimately causes the delay. It is a caution to developers negotiating tight build-to-suit occupancy deadlines to account for every link in the approval chain, including requirements held by separate government bodies entirely.", "judge": "Andre B. Mathis", "tags": ["zoning", "permitting", "section-1983", "michigan", "industrial"]}
